---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import {
  alertAccentTree,
  alertBannerTree,
  alertInlineTree,
  alertNeutralTree,
} from "../../../demos/alert";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;
const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

---

<Base title="Alert" description="Alert: Persistent inline message with tone (including neutral), accessible announcement and React component.">
  <h1>Alert</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Alert is an inline message that remains in the layout for the duration of its condition. Unlike Toast, which is transient and mounted in a floating region, Alert lives in the flow of content.
  </p>

  <h2>Tones</h2>
  <p>
    The tone decides whether the panel is painted with semantic color. <code>info</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code> and <code>danger</code> color the message. Only <code>danger</code> is advertised as <code>role="alert"</code> (assertive); the rest use <code>role="status"</code> (polite).
  </p>
  <p>
    All four read a <strong>feedback role</strong>, never the accent: the tone tells what happened, so it cannot change when the brand changes. <code>info</code> only had his own ramp in decision 26 (<code>docs/decisiones/0026-info-es-un-rol-de-feedback-no-el-acento.md</code>); Before, it read <code>accent</code>, and a magenta mark painted each information notice magenta.
  </p>

  <h3>Neutral</h3>
  <p>
    The default: surface and edge, without semantic painting. It exists for when the color should not be the prominent signal, the text (and, in a <a href="/en/components/toast">Toast</a>, the floating and disappearing) already loads the message. Use it in ordinary notices, short confirmations, or any case where painting the “success” or “info” panel would add urgency that the content does not have.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={alertNeutralTree(t)} label="Alert neutral" />

  <h2>Presentations</h2>
  <p>Presentation decides visual weight, not urgency.</p>

  <h3>Banner</h3>
  <p>The full panel is for a condition that needs a title and explanation next to the affected content.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={alertBannerTree(t)} label="Alert banner" />

  <h3>Accent</h3>
  <p>The sidebar preserves the tone signal and lets a recovery action take center stage.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={alertAccentTree(t)} label="Alert accent" />

  <h3>Inline</h3>
  <p>Without panel or border, a short confirmation accompanies a block that already has its own structure.</p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={alertInlineTree(t)} label="Alert inline" />

  <h2>Anatomy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>sk-alert__icon</code> is decorative and only appears when it provides a visual signal.</li>
    <li><code>sk-alert__content</code> groups optional title and description.</li>
    <li><code>sk-alert__actions</code> hosts actions that belong to the consumer; the optional closure lives there.</li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-alert__dismiss</code> is that closure, authored as an icon-only ghost
      <a href="/en/components/button">Button</a> in size <code>sm</code>, exactly as in
      <a href="/en/components/toast">Toast</a>: it inherits the 44px hit target, the state layer and
      the focus ring, and the part class is what makes the glyph read the tone's color. With no
      visible text, <code>aria-label</code> is required.
    </li>
    <li><code>data-tone</code> accepts <code>neutral</code> (default), <code>info</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>warning</code> or <code>danger</code>.</li>
    <li><code>data-presentation</code> accepts <code>banner</code> (default), <code>accent</code> or <code>inline</code>.</li>
    <li><code>data-dismissible</code> says the closure exists; what it does when used is the binding's.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>
    The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of each example. <code>dismissible</code> decides
    whether the close button exists and <code>onDismiss</code> what it does when used: the structure
    is the contract's, the behaviour is the binding's. Passing only <code>onDismiss</code> also draws
    the button, for code written before <code>dismissible</code> existed.
  </p>
</Base>
